## Workspace layout & linking

Two facts about this workspace that every path and every import depends on. Both are **detected**, by the same resolvers the house generators use — so what this section says and where a generator puts things cannot disagree.

**Layout — where projects live.** {{#layout-shared}}Apps and libraries alike: **`{{APPS_DIR}}/`**.{{/layout-shared}}{{^layout-shared}}Apps: **`{{APPS_DIR}}/`**. Libraries: **`{{LIBS_DIR}}/`**.{{/layout-shared}} Resolved from `nx.json` → `workspaceLayout` first (Nx's own field, so Nx's generators agree with the house's), else inferred from where existing projects already sit (`tools/` never counts — it holds workspace tooling, not what this project ships), else the house default (`apps/` + `packages/`). To change where **new** projects go, change `workspaceLayout` in `nx.json` (nothing existing moves); to place one project elsewhere, pass `--directory`. A house generator given only a name lands in the right directory: `{{NX}} g @bespunky/nx-tools:app --name=<app>` → `{{APPS_DIR}}/<app>`.

{{#linking-paths}}**Linking — `paths`.** Projects are defined by `project.json` and reach each other through **path aliases** in the root tsconfig (`compilerOptions.paths`): a library's import path maps straight to its source entry. There is nothing to declare on the consuming side — the alias is global. The house library generators (`publishable-lib`, `design-system`, `ds-component`) write and maintain the alias; never hand-edit one in.
{{/linking-paths}}{{#linking-workspaces}}**Linking — `workspaces` (a TypeScript-solution workspace).** Projects are **packages**: each is a package-manager workspace member, its `package.json` `name` **is** its import path, its `exports` map that path to source under this workspace's custom condition (`customConditions` in `tsconfig.base.json`, so TypeScript reads the `.ts`, never an unbuilt `dist`), and the root `tsconfig.json` is a solution file that only **references** projects. There are **no tsconfig `paths` aliases** — don't add one. A consumer **declares** each library it imports as a dependency in its own `package.json` (`workspace:*` under pnpm / yarn 2+, `*` under npm / yarn 1) — pnpm links nothing undeclared, and npm or yarn only by the accident of hoisting. The house library generators write all of this; when you add an import by hand, add the dependency too and run `{{NX}} sync` (it keeps project references current).
{{#angular}}
**Angular here is an honest hybrid — read this before generating Angular code.** `@nx/angular` refuses TypeScript-solution workspaces outright, because the Angular compiler does not support TypeScript project references. The house generators (`app`, `publishable-lib`, `ds-component`, …) step past that guard with upstream's own opt-out, `NX_IGNORE_UNSUPPORTED_TS_SETUP`, scoped to a single generator call: Angular **apps** are created as classic `project.json` islands (their own non-composite tsconfigs) that import workspace packages through those packages' `exports`; Angular **libraries** are real workspace packages like any other. So: **generate Angular projects through the house generators** — a raw `{{NX}} g @nx/angular:application|library` here fails with Nx's TS-solution error, and forcing it with the variable yourself creates a library nothing links. The caveat, stated plainly: the opt-out is **undocumented** upstream. If Nx removes it, the house generators fail loudly with Nx's own error (never silently), and the toolkit's fixture test is the tripwire that catches it first.
{{/angular}}{{/linking-workspaces}}
